        },
        "sources": {
            "supplies.plus": "the battery block below",
            "battery": "per AA cell, Energizer E91 (alkaline Zn/MnO2). Capacity: the datasheet's ~2900 mAh at 25 mA continuous to 0.8 V, 21 °C (as quoted by lygte-info.dk 'Comparison of AA battery chemistry'). Curve: estimate shaped like the datasheet's low-drain discharge curve (no plateau: 1.6 V fresh sloping to ~1.1 V at 90 % used, then a knee; 0 % = 0.8 V at the terminals at 25 mA) plus I·R; resistance ~0.15 ohm fresh, about double at half charge, ~0.75 ohm at 90 % used (learningaboutelectronics.com 'Battery internal resistance'; TI SLVA194: rises as the cell empties), steeply higher past the cut-off; points below 0 %: estimate (an alkaline gives only a few % more below 0.8 V). Not verified against a bench measurement: measure your cells",
            "battery.rate": "fitted with tools/fit_battery.py (the simulation's own model) to the E91 continuous-discharge capacities to 0.8 V: 2900 mAh at 25 mA and ~1400 mAh at 500 mA (datasheet, via lygte-info.dk), ~2200 mAh at 100 mA (lygte-info.dk's estimate); rms error 3 % (25 mA -5 %, 100 mA +1 %, 500 mA 0 %). The one-time-constant model (~7 h) gives the slow recovery, not the fast one in the first seconds after a load stops"
        }
    },
